        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: dǎoháng",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dǎoháng — \"to navigate by satnav\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ yòng shǒujī dǎoháng zhǎodào le nà jiā fàndiàn — I found the way to that restaurant with my phone."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dǎoháng is built from dǎo 'to lead' and háng 'to sail', both inherited from Middle Chinese, dawH and hang (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'to lead' and the second 'a boat'. dawH would regularly give dào: the falling-rising tone of dǎo is irregular."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for getting around."
            }
          ]
